  <static seed="1893" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/FractionApplication/FractionDivisionWordProblems30.pg">
    <statement><p>A food bank just received <m>40</m> kilograms of emergency food. Each family in need is to receive <m>{{\frac{5}{12}}}</m> kilograms of food. How many families can be served with the <m>40</m> kilograms of food?</p>
<p><fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/> families can be served with the <m>40</m> kilograms of food.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>96</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>We can address this problem as repeatedly taking away <m>{{\frac{5}{12}}}</m> kilograms from <m>40</m> kilograms, which implies a division problem:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
\begin{aligned}[t]
   40 \div {{\frac{5}{12}}} 
      \amp = \frac{40}{1} \cdot \frac{12}{5}  \\
      \amp = \frac{8}{1} \cdot \frac{12}{1}  \\
      \amp = \frac{8\cdot12}{1 \cdot 1} \\
      \amp = {96}
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p><m>{96}</m> families can be served with the <m>40</m> kilograms of food.</p>
</solution>
